  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067869/assets/bebb7c4ca5e38d75136146f6e4547b0e72d529ce24453a3d0212bca1ffbfe4c0.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the area\n\n$$\nA=xy\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 3**: This is subject to the constraint that the perimeter is $11.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\n2x+2y=11.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =11 \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{11}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x(\\frac{11}{2}-x) \\\\ A & =\\frac{11}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{\u2032}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{11}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{\u2032}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2033}(x)=-2\\,\u27f9\\,{A}^{\u2033}(\\frac{11}{4})=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{\u2033}(\\frac{11}{4})$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{11}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{11}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy={(\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}\u22487.56.\n$$",
